\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1647866 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 Chapter 8 \endash  Doubt Creeps In
\par 
\par I was in the crumbling church again. Everything was less real, this time\'85
 nothing made sense any more. Roses grew from between the cracks in the walls, blooming life surrounded me, despite the fact that drifts of snow fell in through the decaying roof. The gales that r
attled the broken panes of stained glass chilled me through to my core, but my chest\'85 burned. When I looked down, my heart glowed through my ribs like a kiln.
\par I tore my shirt up over my head, and it dissolved into ash. Cuneiform lit like embers from amidst my fur, in a familiar pattern. The Heart of Faith, its inscription, burned into my flesh. 
\par I clawed at my chest, desperately, to no avail. I felt like it was blackening my insides with its fire, and soon I began to cough. Smoke, acrid and sooty, and the stench of burning flesh.
\par \'93I can relieve you of that.\'94
\par I looked to the altar, and there he stood. Not in a gown this time, but a loose-flowing shirt and breeches, the top open to reveal his flat, downy-furred chest. 
\par I fell to my hands and knees, and crawled towards the altar. He watched me the whole while, the stained glass behind him shifting between the phases of the moon, and the sun and stars, throwing his figure into stark relief.
\par When I at last grew near, he slipped down to his knees and reached for me. His palm cupped my cheek and settled against my jaw, his fingers carding through the fur at the ruff of my neck. It felt familiar\'85 like it belonged.
\par \'93This will hurt,\'94 he warned me gently, \'93but I must take that from you. It\rquote s killing you.\'94
\par He reached down, pressing his palm flat against my chest, and began to push. I could not stop coughing, my eyes were full of tears, the ash of my burning body filling my throat. 
\par His fingers pressed past the edge of my skin, sinking into my chest. The burn grew, and intensified, like it would flare out before he ripped it from me. 
\par I couldn\rquote t breathe. I was dying, charring from the inside out, my own heart was destroying me. I could still see him there leaning over me, somehow, clearly. His gaze was intense, focused\'85 and then closer. Closer still.
\par His muzzle crossed the distance between us, and slotted in against mine, meeting, somehow\'85 so perfectly. He breathed.
\par And then I breathed. And it was air, not smoke, filling my lungs. His breath, in me. Resuscitating me. 
\par His hand was deeper now, wrist buried past my ribs. He dug for the Heart, closing his grasping fingers \lquote round it. The fire fought back, burning up the air he had given me. But he only gave me more.
 I could feel the heat licking between us, drawn through my muzzle into his. His body began to glow\'85
\par 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1647866 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
\par \tab I dragged in a ragged breath and rolled over to cough grotesquely, deep and wet, and absurdly loud. My head throbbed, my chest ached, my throat clenched around an un-scratchable itch.
\par \tab But I was awake.
\par \tab I was awake, and alive. 
\par \tab It wasn\rquote t just the dream that had woken me thinking I was struggling to breathe\'85
 I was genuinely struggling to breathe. My cough was back with a vengeance, my head felt fit to roll off my shoulders, and as soon as my memory returned to me, my heart sunk into a depressive pit. 
\par \tab I wasn\rquote t sure where I was. The room was dark and unfamiliar, and I was alone. But I recalled the last moments I\rquote d been awake and the whole of the encounter that had come before. I remembered Malachi speaking to me as my consciousness
 waned\'85 I remembered what I said to him.
\par \tab God forgive me. I remembered what I said to him.
\par \tab The door opened, a hulking shadow stepping inside. As if I\rquote d summoned him, the Inquisitor walked into the room, lighting an oil lamp with a candle he was carrying before snuffing the wick on the candle and moving towards me.
\par \tab I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid5647886 had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  summoned him, I supposed. With my raucous, uncontrollable coughing. 
\par \tab \'93How long were you traveling through this weather without shelter or all-weather garb?\'94 he asked distractedly, as he poured something from a small flask into a steaming clay mug he\rquote d brought in with him. \'93
Your dedication does you credit, Brother\'85 but you\rquote ll be of no use to anyone if you\rquote re dead. Why didn\rquote t you tell me you\rquote d been ill?\'94
\par \tab \'93Just,\'94 I wheezed, \'93a cold. And I was,\'94 I coughed, \'93past\'85 the worst of it.\'94
\par \tab \'93It is lingering in your lungs, according to the Physician,\'94 he muttered, moving over towards the bed and setting the cup down beside me on an end table. He did not treat me like a child or patronize me. He pulled u
p a chair and sat beside me, waiting to see if I could do it myself.
\par \tab I forced myself up to a sitting position, wincing as my head throbbed at the change of angle. I felt\'85 bad\'85 but not like I was dying. Just sick, with a painful bump on my head.
\par \tab \'93I get lingering coughs, often,\'94 I told him, reaching for the drink he\rquote d set down. I sniffed it, but was unable to discern what it was just from the steam. I began to sip from it all the same.
\par \tab \'93It\rquote s just thin tea, honey, lemon juice and whiskey,\'94 he informed me.
\par \tab I nearly coughed it up, peering over the rim at him and wiping my muzzle with the back of my hand. \'93This has alcohol in it?\'94
\par \tab He looked concerned. \'93I\rquote m sorry. It\rquote s a country remedy. Have you taken a vow of sobriety? I should have asked.\'94
\par \tab \'93No,\'94 I answered. \'93Just\'85 I think my tolerance is bad.\'94
\par \tab \'93Oh, Lord,\'94 he chuckled. \'93Nothing to worry about there, I assure you. Barely a capful. Just does wonders for colds, with the right mix of ingredients.\'94
\par \tab \'93I thought it was medicine,\'94 I said.
\par \tab \'93Well, it is,\'94 he shrugged. \'93Country medicine. It\rquote s all the Physician would offer you in a bottle, I just mixed it myself is all.\'94
\par \tab \'93Thank you,\'94 I said quietly. 
\par \tab This was intolerable. This man was quite literally everything I wanted to be in life. And thanks to my humiliating failure, he was forced to play nursemaid to me.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1647866 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 \'93Before you go fretting, set yourself at ease,
\'94 he said, standing and walking towards the one window in the room and pulling back the curtain for a moment, revealing a dark blue sky illuminated by the faint yello
w glow of a lantern, somewhere nearby. Heavy white drifts blew in fierce, diagonal lines across the one window square I could see, before he let the curtain drop. 
\par \'93First blizzard of the year,\'94 he said, shaking out his thick fur as he adjusted the robe around his shoulders. \'93The Heart Thief won\rquote t be leaving the city. Not in this. At least not until the roads are cleared. It moved in fast too, so\'85
 I don\rquote t see how he\rquote d have had time to arrange transport. And we found his belongings. At least those he left at the Lane house.\'94
\par He settled himself back down in the chair, heavily. \'93He\rquote s trapped,\'94 he assured me. \'93We\rquote ll find him.\'94
\par I clutched the warm mug in both paws, taking stock of myself and my surroundings fully for the first time. I was in a bedroom\'85 likely his, judging by the scant few possessions I saw around the room. It was clear this apartment was kept up, but didn
\rquote t see much use. From outside it had looked very small, and the size of the bedroom confirmed that. There was probably a sitting room and a wash r
oom if he was lucky, but possibly not even that. He might have taken his baths at the Church, since it was so close.
\par Certainly, this place was no family home.
\par \'93You live alone?\'94 I asked, then immediately realized I should not be probing his personal life.
\par I apparently couldn\rquote t help myself. I asked stupid questions. It seemed to be my lot in life.
\par He didn\rquote t seem to mind the query. \'93Aye,\'94 he answered. \'93I go where I\rquote m needed by the Ministry, I\rquote ve always been willing to travel for my work, and I\rquote ve just never seen fit to\'85 settle. Not yet, anyway. Since I 
move around so much, felt cruel to consider starting a family.\'94 He\rquote d been staring off at the window, but he regarded me when he asked, casually, \'93You?\'94
\par \'93I\rquote m nineteen,\'94 I said, by way of an answer.
\par \'93I knew Abbey kids who got married at thirteen,\'94 he said. \'93Not common, but\'85 it happens. Especially the girls.\'94 He scratched his claws through his neck fur, idly. \'93I\rquote 
m twenty-eight, by the way. Before you ask. So, you can stop addressing me like I\rquote m your Father. We\rquote re not so far apart.\'94
\par \'93We\rquote re several lifetimes apart,\'94 I sighed, thumping my head back against the wall behind me, and immediately regretting it. \'93At least insofar as experience, and standing.\'94 I clutched at where I was wrapped up, where I\rquote 
d nearly knocked myself out, falling.
\par \'93You\rquote ve got a good }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid15008178 mind}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 , though,\'94 he said, pointing a claw at me. \'93That\rquote s a gift. Won
\rquote t take long for you to rise in the ranks, trust me. You\rquote ll be my superior in five years or less.\'94\tab 
\par  \'93Heaven forbid,\'94 I muttered.
\par \'93You need to work on your confidence,\'94 he said as he lifted the same flask he\rquote d poured a bit of into my cup, to his own muzzle. He let out a breath after he\rquote d swallowed, sounding satisfied with whatever was in there. \'93
As an Inquisitor you need to put forth an unflappable aura of authority, or you\rquote ll get walked all over. No one spills their secrets unless they\rquote re utterly certain you\rquote 
ll pry them out of their skull one way or another anyway. If you can\rquote t rely on intimidation, you at least need to appear certain in your convictions. Preferably both, but one or the other will do. Is your faith in God strong, Isidor?\'94
\par The way he asked it\'85 it sounded like an actual question. As if I could feel any other way.
\par \'93Of course,\'94 I said.
\par \'93Well, then trust that He knows what He\rquote s doing,\'94 he reasoned. \'93God put you on this path because\'85
 well, because you seem to be good at it. He guided you to the very place you are now. Trust in Him, if not yourself. And use that faith to strengthen your own confidence.\'94
\par I mulled that over in my head. It made a sort of sense. God was infinitely wise, and while I was not so arrogant as to believe He directed my life in all ways- we had the freedom to make our own mistakes and go against His will, after
 all- the circumstances of my birth, my dietary restrictions, and the poverty that had led my family to give me up, were not decisions any of us had made. And a perfect example of how what can seem to be misfortune is the intentional will of God, leading 
one to their proper purpose in life. If not for any of that, my family would never have given me up to Ebon Gables, and I would not be serving in my role in the Church now, doing this most important task.
\par It had to be His will, and if He wanted me here, in this moment, serving Him thusly, that meant He had faith that I was capable of this. I could do this. 
\par I needed to have less doubt. Of His will, and in myself.
\par I felt my muzzle tick up, incrementally. Malachi must have noticed, because he gave a slight smile as well. \'93Did that help?\'94
\par \'93Yes,\'94 I said, gratefully. I turned towards him, clutching the blankets at my waist and fighting back a tremble of joy. \'93You have no idea how much. Thank you.\'94
\par He smiled more broadly. \'93Always glad to see the fire come back to a Brother\rquote s eyes. You\rquote re good at this, lad. Trust me. I\rquote ve looked over your work, and so has the Ministry. There\rquote 
s a reason they had enough faith in your investigation to recall me here to help you.\'94
\par \'93Well, that might also have meant they assumed I wasn\rquote t up to the task on my own,\'94 I sighed, my claws curling at my thighs. \'93To be fair\'85 that\rquote s accurate.\'94
\par \'93I have resources, experience and authority you don\rquote t,\'94 he waved a paw. \'93That isn\rquote t your fault. You did the research and the legwork. Now\rquote s the chase. This bitch is just a slippery one, with a network we haven\rquote 
t penetrated yet. We\rquote ll hunt him down, you and I. And then you can help me crack him open. Channel some of that frustration of yours into something useful, eh?\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid13903511 

\par  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
My throat closed up and I was seized by another fit of coughing. Each hack made my vision go white and my head throb, but I felt the bed shift as Malachi moved beside me and the heavy slap of his paw on my back. I wasn\rquote 
t sure if it helped any, really\'85 but he left his rough, calloused palm there after the coughing had subsided, and that }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid9072221 was}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  a comfort. I wasn\rquote t really sure why, I just enjoyed the weight of it. The connection that came from it.
\par I hadn\rquote t realized how very badly I\rquote d wanted something like that, or for how long, until just then.
\par We sat in companionable silence for a time, until he spoke again. \'93Do you remember what you told me as we left that courtyard at the summer home, Brother Isidor?\'94
\par I froze, and subconsciously bent forward, shying away from his touch. I felt the sharp edge of his claws, not digging but pressing against my shoulder, holding me in place.
\par \'93Calm,\'94 he eased, in that level but commanding baritone. Then, \'93I take it that means you do?\'94
\par I swallowed. \'93I need to make Confession.\'94
\par He didn\rquote t respond immediately. But I felt the bed shift again and he stood, the cutting line of his gaze drawing my own up to it, offering no alternative. He could compel without words.
\par This is what I wanted to be. An Inquisitor. Right now, I felt a canyon\rquote s width apart from him. How did I dare I aspire to this? I didn\rquote t have the presence, the nerve, I was a basket case of emotions and melodrama, I was-
\par \'93There is that doubt again,\'94 he rumbled and opened his palms, offering them to me. \'93Come, Brother. It is obvious you need this, to clear your head. Let me be of service.\'94
\par I gaped up at him. Was he saying..?
\par \'93You want me to give Confession\'85 here? To you?\'94 I stammered.
\par \'93In a sense, you already have,\'94 he reasoned. \'93But if you want to clear yourself with God, let me be the vessel for your redemption in His eyes.\'94
\par \'93But you\rquote re not\'85\'94 I mumbled.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7818247 A Priest.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 Even Father Helstrom had taken his vows as a Father of our Parish when he\rquote d retired from the Ministry. Templar were many things, but Priests, they were not. Some wore the honorific of 
\lquote Father\rquote  because of their age or seniority, but there was a big difference between the vows and the authority bestowed between Priests, and Templar. Primarily for reasons of Confession, and what remained Sacrosanct\'85
 held in trust between only the Priest, the Sinner, and God. 
\par \'93Brother, if I am permitted to tear confession from the mouths of the unwilling,\'94 he said without a trace of regret, \'93I fail to see how God would disapprove of me taking it from those who freely give it. The vows, the titles\'85 they\rquote re
 pomp. You have my word everything you say will remain between the two of us, and the Almighty.\'94
\par He leaned down and plucked my hands from my lap, wrapping them in his own slightly larger ones. Our hand paws
 were similar colors, mine white, his a light cream, but his claws stood in stark contrast to my own, black, and longer. Inquisitors were not bound by the rules of Court Etiquette to trim back their claws or cover their teeth in public. 
\par He leaned in closer to me. \'93All that matters, as I see it,\'94 he spoke, \'93is whether or not you trust me.\'94
\par By doctrine, this was wrong. He was wrong. Every logical part of my mind and my faith told me as much.
\par But I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid14094912 did}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
 trust him. And how could I lie about that, when I was literally about to give Confession? Lying, especially to an Inquisitor, was a sin.
\par He stayed standing over me, holding my palms in his. We were supposed to be at eye level, if we really intended to do this, but that would literally mean sitting in bed with me. Which\'85 he\rquote 
d been doing, a moment ago, and there was also the chair, but-
\par \'93Isidor.\'94
\par I closed my eyes and just nodded.
\par There was the briefest of pauses and then, his deep voice began the prayer. \'93God, hear through mine ears, the willing testimony of sin from this, your son. Relieve him of the burden of bearing it alone, and show him the way to redemption.\'94
\par I opened my eyes, staring down at our joined hands. For some reason, I couldn\rquote t bear to look up at him. But then, given what I was about to say, I could hardly be expected to.
\par \'93Tell me about these dreams,\'94 he prompted.
\par My heart pounded against my ribs. My paws were shaking. I had never felt so anxious, so frightened, so utterly unprepared to give Confession. Was it because it was him? Or because of what I was going to say?
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid4395538 He already knew}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 , I reminded myself. I just had to beg forgiveness from God, now. I had to.
\par \'93Sometimes,\'94 I said around a dry tongue, wetting my mouth as well as I could to get the words out, \'93sometimes I\rquote m in\'85 a church. A crumbling place, and I-I don\rquote t think\'85 I\rquote ve ever actually been there. It\rquote 
s familiar but unfamiliar. I feel as though I am\'85 meant? To be there? Like coming home, but\'85 it\rquote s not home.\'94
\par \'93Do you think this dream is a vision of some sort, then?\'94 he asked, his voice steady. Not pushing me, but allowing me to follow the obvious train of thought there, if I wanted to.
\par \'93I don\rquote t- no,\'94 I said solidly. \'93I might have\'85 perhaps\'85 but not given what happens, there. God would send me no such visions. And who am I to receive such direct guidance from Him? No. They are a creation of my mind, I\rquote 
m certain. A collage of comforting memories, meant to self-satisfy, to pave my path towards the things I desire. To justify my sins to myself.\'94
\par I couldn\rquote t see him, but I felt, heard him hum, thoughtfully. \'93How introspective, Isidor. Not many can peer inwards and untangle our own justifications thusly. I think God would appreciate that you are so willing to question yourself, rathe
r than buy into the notions that would most please you. Good. Continue.\'94
\par I blinked, momentarily stunned. I couldn\rquote t remember ever being complimented while I gave Confession, before. How queer.
\par \'93It\rquote s not always the church,\'94 I said, my ears falling. \'93Sometimes it\rquote s a bed, a room- not necessarily the one I fell asleep in- and many times I even realize I\rquote m dreaming. But I still cannot seem to stop myself.\'94 
\par I formed the words a few times, having trouble, but he gave me the space to continue. \'93She\'85 he\'85 is there. Almost always. Before I\rquote d met them, when I had dreams like this, they were always\'85
 half-formed. It was never a specific person. But now, it\rquote s-I- there is no doubt. It\rquote s\'85\'94
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid14365394 Darcy}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 .
\par \'93The Heart Thief,\'94 I said, bitterly. \'93I do not know why they have such a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid16063377 grip}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid16063377 upon}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  my unconscious mind.\'94
\par \'93Perhaps you\rquote re attracted to them.\'94
\par \'93I\'85\'94 God. He was right, of course. It was the most obvious conclusion. But\'85 \'93I mean, not consciously,\'94 I insisted. \'93I would never- could never- that is to say, you could not really make a good faith argument against the fact that\'85
 they }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid14365394 are}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  attractive. Beautiful, even. But I don\rquote t }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid15074744 want}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  to be attracted to them.\'94
\par \'93I could make that argument,\'94 he muttered. \'93But as you said, it hardly matters. The fact is, it is distressingly common, attraction outside one\rquote s own kind. Common, but we must remember\'85 unnatural.\'94
\par I nodded, \'93I know,\'94 I whispered.
\par \'93But contending with unnatural attraction is one of life\rquote s many tests,\'94 he continued. \'93As long as we are willing to recognize when we are broken, we can begin to mend. These\'85
 flaws in our logical minds, in our sinful bodies, may be life-long afflictions. God does not expect us to be perfect. He expects us to fight our hardest }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid4656309 for}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  that purity of spirit, even if that means we lose a few battles along the way.\'94
\par \'93I don\rquote t know how to win this battle,\'94 I said, quietly but desperately. \'93It only seems to be getting worse. I\rquote ve had dreams like this infrequently since I was fourteen, but they are }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid354892 so}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  much more vivid now, and the hold they have over me is strengthening. They have grown more and more\'85\'94 I stammered, \'93\'85 obscene, a-and\'85
 when I wake\'85\'94
\par \'93What do you do in these dreams?\'94 he asked, tone implacable. Not answering the man, even by half, was not an option.
\par It took me too long to respond. Much too long. But somehow, eventually, I managed. \'93We touch one another,\'94 I said, voice threadbare. \'93We\'85 put our muzzles on one another, and I put them beneath me, and I-I\'85 we\'85\'94
 I hung my head, shame shrinking my entire body down. \'93\'85 we fornicate. And when I wake, the evidence of my sin is upon my person. It has gone beyond my unconscious mind, now. I am losing control over my body. It betrays me.\'94
\par \'93Your body is no separate person you can blame, Isidor,\'94 he said. \'93It is yours to command.\'94
\par \'93It doesn\rquote t feel that way sometimes\'85\'94 I whispered.
\par \'93Sin is not always within our ability to control,\'94 he said, \'93at least not in its entirety. We falter, we fall to desire, inevitably\'85 but we can recognize it. We can repent. Do you know why we sin, Isidor?\'94
\par \'93Because it is mortal to sin,\'94 I replied, automatically.
\par \'93Because,\'94 he gripped my palms tighter, \'93we are broken.\'94
\par I nodded, slowly.
\par \'93Admitting it to yourself deprives your ego of its shield. It is important that we have no pride when we stand before God in Judgment. Pride is utilitarian, it exists so that we may be made useful\'85
 it is not a defense against sin. That is why we make Confession. Now. It is important to speak the words aloud.\'94
\par There was silence, and I realized he was waiting for me.
\par \'93I am\'85\'94 I said, finally looking up, \'93\'85broken.\'94
\par He nodded at me, keeping my gaze. \'93Yes.\'94
\par \'93I am ravaged by sin,\'94 I said, sniffing. \'93I am weak before the temptations of the flesh. I want to win this battle, Brother. I want this to\'85\'94 I dragged in another long breath, and let it out with a shudder. \'93
I want it to stop. Please, God. Take this burden from me, or help strengthen me in the fight for my soul.\'94
\par \'93In these dreams of yours, Brother,\'94 he asked, \'93is the Heart Thief a woman, or a man?\'94
\par The question stopped me dead. 
\par It had come so suddenly, I had been in the middle of my plea to God\'85 but really, it was the next logical thing to ask, wasn\rquote t it? Because, it mattered.
\par It mattered.
\par The severity of the sin was at issue, here. To have these sorts of feelings, to imagine these types of activities with a woman was bad enough. But with a man\'85
\par \'93I didn\rquote t even know they were a man until yesterday,\'94 I breathed.
\par A beat. And then, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7481548 knowingly}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 , he }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7481548 knew}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  just by the sound of my voice-! He said, \'93That is not what I asked, Brother.\'94
\par The answer was undeniable. I couldn\rquote t }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7481548 lie}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  to him- what would be the point in all of this?!
\par I couldn\rquote t lie to God. He already knew.
\par \'93Both,\'94 I uttered, the words leaving my tongue burning like they were bile. I was disgusting. I was unsalvageable. All that talk of having faith in myself, of building up my confidence, and now he\rquote d know, and he\rquote d-
\par Oh, God. What did the Order do to Heretics? It varied. It could be anything from a public shaming, to corporal punishment, to excommunication. It could be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7481548 death}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 .
\par Would they really consider any of those for something that had only happened in my head, though?
\par \'93What did you do with him?\'94
\par I lifted my head again, quickly, only to find him still staring down at me with that cool expression. I wanted to ask \lquote why does it matter?\rquote  It was all sin. 
\par \'93We\'85 kissed,\'94 I said, with difficulty.
\par He waited, and then eventually asked, \'93Is that all?\'94
\par \'93With him, I\'85 yes,\'94 I babbled, \'93she was always a woman in my dreams before then. That\rquote s\'85 when I dreamt of her, that\rquote s when\'85 the most lascivious things happened. But, Brother, what if it gets worse? What if\'85
 what if I keep having these dreams, and he is in them, and-\'93
\par \'93Do you know how two men sin together, Isidor?\'94 He asked, point blank.
\par Finally, at the very least, my honesty did not condemn me. \'93I\'85 not really,\'94 I admitted. \'93I\rquote ve never\'85 thought about it\'85 much.\'94
\par He looked me over. It felt like he was staring }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid13174956 through}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  me, discerning- I suppose-
 whether or not I was lying. In that at least, I felt confident. I had no earthly idea what two men would do with their bodies that was meant to be done between a man and a woman. Kissing, I suppose. But I knew the depravity could run deeper than that. 

\par This wasn\rquote t a good line of thought to linger on.
\par \'93Good,\'94 he said, finally. \'93Best you not get an education. There is learning in this world that is useful, Brother, and learning that will pave your road to damnation. Better to remain in the dark, on some things.\'94
\par A pounding rap so loud it went off in the a
partment like a gunshot snapped both of us to attention. It repeated again in quick succession several times, and it took me getting my bearings to realize it was coming from the door into the residence, just outside of this room.
\par Malachi whipped his head around to stare at the door with a curl of his lip and a flash of fang in the dark, his countenance gone defensive as a dyre guarding their den. His fur stood on end, and he let out a frustrated growl in his throat.
\par \'93I apologize for this, Brother Isidor, but we must continue later,\'94 he said as he released my hands and went over to a hook at the corner of the room, where his blade was stowed.
\par I had never in my life been interrupted or let go of by someone giving me Confession. I stared down at my palms, not certain what to do.
\par \'93Who in the bloody hell demands my time in the middle of a goddamn snowstorm?\'94 he grumbled aloud, after quickly cinching the sword on his belt. I murmured a brief prayer of forgiveness for him, for swearing as he did. Let alone in 
such a holy moment. But I understood the frustration, acutely.
\par I forced myself out of bed, realized abruptly that I was only wearing my smallclothes, and searched about for my clothing. I found it folded neatly beside me on the end table and went about dressing as fast as I could while Malachi answered the door.

\par Even from inside the bedroom, I could see how he pressed his ear to the door first, then checked through the peep hole, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid6378530 then}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  in the same way he\rquote d answered the door to me, opened it halfway.
\par \'93Explain yourself,\'94 was how he greeted whoever was there, firm, commanding, as he\rquote d spoken to me when we first met. Just a hint of latent intimidation.
\par \'93Are you Inquisitor Malachi?\'94 an unfamiliar voice asked- nay, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid11823553 demanded}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 . 
His accent was refined, he could\rquote ve been a Pedigree, but it was still astonishing to me that he could speak with such a demanding tone to Malachi. 
I could only vaguely make out their shadows from the half-open doorway into the bedroom. The man sounded canine, looked to be far shorter than Malachi, and he had something in his hand. That\rquote s all I could make out.
\par \'93Aye Sir, I am,\'94 Malachi responded, calmly. \'93You\rquote d have no reason to be here unless you already knew that.\'94
\par I craned my neck out the doorway while I tugged up my trousers, and the man\rquote s face finally came into focus. Obscured by drifts of snow (because Malachi wasn\rquote t allowing him inside), but discernable.
\par He was a Red Setter, or at least that\rquote s what the Pedigree Society called his kind. A head shorter than me and even more so compared to Malac
hi, build rather average from what I could tell, with the long rust-colored fur draping off his angular face brushed and styled in handsome, loose waves. He had extremely light brown eyes, like honey,
 that stood out even more so when in the direct glow of a lantern. He was dressed well, wrapped in a silk scarf, wearing a dark coat and a fine hat, and he had a cane with a metal grip, held in one hand. Likely what he\rquote 
d used to rap on the door with.
\par And the man looked\'85 furious. Composed, making an effort to remain even-tempered, but I could feel his outrage from a hall away. When he spoke again, his voice reflected it.
\par \'93What Parish are you with? Are you even with the local Ministry?\'94 He snapped. \'93Who\rquote s your handler? What cause was there for an Investigation- what }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid3888505 possible}
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  cause could there be- into my Great Aunt and Uncle Lane?\'94
\par Without moving his gaze off the man, Malachi reached aside to the coat hook on the wall near the front door, where a small leather bag hung. I had one just like it that attached to my belt.
\par \'93I am Malachi of Redcoven, I am a Son of the Redcoven Parish, Saint Rhine\rquote s, and my handler here in the city is one Father Whitfield, of Saint Rhine\rquote s,\'94
 he produced something from his pouch then, creased and aged, and folded into quarters. His patents. His had }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid1329072 far}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  more seals on them than mine had.
\par \'93And your business with my extended family?\'94 the man asked after snapping the paper out of Malachi\rquote s hand and looking it over, heedless of the snow falling on it.
\par \'93Let\rquote s not play coy,\'94 Malachi drawled, his voice sinking through the floor to barely register as anything more than a growl. He took his patents back, cocked his other
 arm in the doorway and leaned down, and forward. The stranger held firm until he was inches from bumping noses with him, then was forced to move back and away.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid1329072 Discomfort them, force them to take a submissive posture.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 \'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid1329072 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 I had no official Inquisitor training yet, and if the Ministry did decide to accept me in time, I\rquote d have to go through nearly two years of specialized instruction before I\rquote 
d be permitted to act as one. But once, Brother Dolus had sat me down and given me what he\rquote d called a \lquote precursor course\rquote  in interrogation tactics, just in case I\rquote d need it while pursuing my investigation. 
\par \'93Cillian Rathborne,\'94 Malachi clucked out the man\rquote s name, slowly.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7498024 \'93Use their name. Use it as an accusation.\'94
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 The Setter did not refute him. \'93You cannot,\'94 he breathed out through his obvious rage, \'93just enter one of my homes, unannounced, uninvited, and interrogate my family-\'93
\par \'93Oh, let\rquote s not do this,\'94 Malachi tutted, still pressing the man back with his very presence. \'93This dance. I\rquote 
ve seen your entire file son, you know this waltz well. Believe me when I say, you will not bribe or cajole your way out of this one.\'94
\par He moved suddenly, pressing his advantage against the man until the both of them were outside, and grabbed his cane so hard, he yanked him forward with it.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7498024 \'93Use your body to speak louder than your words can. You can damage resolve far before it becomes necessary to damage flesh.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 \'93Where is he?\'94 Malachi rumbled.
\par \'93You are bound by the Crown Ministry Agreement to contact the Patriarch of a line first, by registered courier, before you interrogate other family members,\'94 the man- Cillian, he\rquote 
d all but admitted to it- said. He was gritting his teeth, his breath puffing out his nose in pants, but he was standing his ground, to his credit. He seemed liable to fall off-balance though\'85 I think he needed that cane.
\par \'93Mister Lane is the Patriarch of the Lane household,\'94 Malachi said. \'93I asked to speak to him first. He\rquote ll tell you as much.\'94
\par \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7881808 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  am the Patriarch of my extended family, since my father and my uncle\rquote s demise,\'94
 he growled out. \'93Mr. and Mrs. Lane are in my care. You must contact }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7881808 me}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
 before any and all investigations into the Lane family.\'94
\par Malachi only scratched his chin. \'93Send your complaint to the Ministry.\'94
\par \'93If you saw my file,\'94 Cillian snarled, \'93then you already knew the Lanes were my wards. You did this }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7881808 willfully}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 !\'94 
\par \'93Do you think,\'94 Malachi gestured vaguely with a paw, \'93this impresses anyone, lad? This right here?\'94
\par He let go of Cillian\rquote s cane quite suddenly at that, and the man stumbled back, catching himself on the railing. I winced, only glad he hadn\rquote t fallen. The stairs here were narrow and precarious.
\par \'93You don\rquote t frighten me,\'94 the Setter said in a simmering tone, after he\rquote d steadied himself. \'93I\rquote ve dealt with your Ministry before, far worse than you, and I\rquote m not }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid11631455 impressed}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  by a man who bullies old people.\'94 
\par \'93The longer you shelter this criminal,\'94 Malachi said, \'93the greater the torment the Ministry will put you through. Where have you got him hidden away, now? Do you really think we won\rquote 
t sniff out his rank stench again? And again? Spare yourself, and him, the terror of an extended hunt. This doesn\rquote t end well for you, the path you\rquote re choosing-\'93
\par \'93Who is he?!\'94 Cillian suddenly demanded, noticing me behind Malachi. The nobleman pointed at me, stabbing his blunt claw in my direction. \'93Him? That\rquote s the young one, isn\rquote t it? The whelp
 who threatened one of my servants outside? You!\'94
\par I went still, correcting myself from stepping back, but only barely. He continued to shake his finger in my direction, trying to walk back up to the door before Malachi\rquote s arm cut him off. It didn\rquote t stop his angry tirade at me, though.
\par \'93Who are you? I want to see }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid3888505 his}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  patents, too. Who do you think you are, drawing steel on my staff?!\'94

\par \'93He\rquote s \lquote staff\rquote  now, is he?\'94 Malachi chuckled humorlessly. 
\par \'93The person you cornered on the patio is my servant,\'94 Cillian insisted vehemently. \'93You frightened them off into a bloody snowstorm, after making wild accusations! Don\rquote t think I\rquote 
m some fool you can run roughshod over, I know how these investigations must be conducted by the rule of law. I\rquote ve got three Solicitors on permanent salary, I will make your life }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid5191805 hell}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 , you cur!\'94
\par He turned his ire back towards me, sticking out his hand again. \'93You! Patents! NOW!\'94
\par Malachi knew I had no patents, but honestly even if I had, they probably wouldn\rquote t have been enough for this man. Or enough, period, by the law. I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid5191805 wasn\rquote t}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  an Inquisitor.
\par \'93My Brother here was never in your house-\'93
\par \'93He was on my grounds! Off the public road, past the gate, without }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid5191805 anyone}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  knowing he was there!\'94
 the man cried. 
\par \'93Which is why he caught the criminal you were sheltering,\'94 Malachi stated.
\par This is why he\rquote d wanted me outside. I didn\rquote t understand the law very well, since I hadn\rquote t been through my training yet. But I\rquote d felt in my gut we\rquote d been doing things wrongly.
\par Cillian was trembling, noticeable mostly in his ears. He was staring only at me now, for some reason. And when he spoke again, it was leveled at me.
\par \'93You stay away from them,\'94 he said, the tips of his fangs glinting white as he spoke the words, lowly.  Showing fangs as a Pedigree was a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid13174956 huge}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  break in decorum. \'93Do you hear me?\'94
\par He\rquote d spoken to Darcy. The look in his eyes said it all. He\rquote d }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid9246851 spoken}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  to Darcy. He knew }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid9246851 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  was the one who\rquote d been chasing them all this while.
\par \'93Sir Rathborne?\'94 Malachi\rquote s voice brought his gaze briefly back to him, and away from me. \'93Tell us where he is,\'94 Malachi growled, \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid9246851 now}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 . Or I will personally request to be your Interrogator when the time comes. And I promise you,\'94 his eyes slipped to slim blue crescents, \'93I will not stop once I\rquote 
ve gotten the answers I want. I will not stop when I am satisfied. I will put in enough time, taking you apart, that even I will grow bored of it. And still, I will not stop.\'94
\par \'93You\rquote re a bloody lunatic,\'94 Cillian rasped, through his teeth.
\par \'93Don\rquote t test me, young lord,\'94 Malachi clucked, almost fondly. \'93I don\rquote t honestly like breaking Pedigrees. Too much paperwork afterwards. Make all of our lives a bit easier, would you?\'94
\par Cillian\rquote s breath steamed out into the air, and he stared up at the towering canine. He gripped his cane a bit tighter, leaned up, and said, \'93Go to hell.\'94
\par Malachi slammed the door in his face. I couldn\rquote t see him in the moments following, but I watched his shoulders slump back down, saw the wind go out of his sails. He didn\rquote t look dismayed, just\'85 relaxing his posture.
\par When he turned to look at me, his features had schooled themselves back into the more congenial demeanor he\rquote d had with me since yesterday.
\par \'93Back to resting for you, Brother. I\rquote ll handle this.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7881808 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1647866 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 \tab We didn\rquote t talk about my Confession.
\par \tab Not for the entire day following. Granted, I spent much of it in bed, save a few trips to tend to my personal needs, wash the road off, and have another two meals down in the Archives with Malachi. We didn\rquote 
t talk about it then, either. Not a word.
\par \tab And I suppose I\rquote 
d been expecting to? It was like waiting for the axe to fall. Never in my life had I given Confession- let alone admitted to such a transgression against God- without being instructed on some means for absolution. Even if it was just extra prayers
 or duties. Something.
\par \tab }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid8010620 Anything}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 .
\par \tab Malachi asked nothing of me. We didn\rquote 
t even finish the prayer together. I eventually did so on my own, begging forgiveness from God without the aid of a Priest, because I was desperate to tie off the loose end the night had left me with. It felt inadequate. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid8010620 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  felt inadequate.
\par \tab But also, humiliatingly, relieved.
\par \tab Which made the guilt bite all the harder, and I knew I deserved to feel that, as well!
\par \tab I felt too many things to name. It was all a tangle. My thoughts would wind and unwind throughout my solitary hours in that bed, going over everything I\rquote d said, trying to understand why Malachi wasn\rquote 
t angry with me. Or if he was, when he\rquote d choose to express it. Maybe he was just disappointed. Maybe he was going to turn me in\'85
\par \tab He was an Inquisitor. It was literally his duty to do so.
\par \tab But would he?
\par \tab Something about the whole of it felt wrong in a way that had nothing to do with the technicality of giving Confession to a man who hadn\rquote t taken the right oaths. It was his insistence, his patience with me, how he\rquote d know
n to ask every right (wrong) question. It had felt\'85 sheltering? Like he\rquote d wanted to give me safe harbor. That\rquote s what Confession was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid6503040 supposed}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  to be, so why did it set me so ill at ease?
\par \tab }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid6503040 Because it had felt like a secret}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 .
\par \tab We could keep no secrets from God obviously, and we had in fact called upon Him to listen. But it almost felt like Malachi had offered to take my confession to keep my secrets between the two of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid12866453 us}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 here in the mortal world.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1647866 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid12866453 \'93You don\rquote 
t tell anyone else that, ever. You hear me? Not ever. Not even a Priest.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 Why did it feel like he\rquote d said that? Had he said something like that? Why couldn\rquote t I remember, exactly?
\par I wanted it all to come to a head, somehow. I needed to know what came next. But bringing it up was impossible, and Malachi wa
s hardly in the apartment over the course of the next day. He hustled in and out, checking in on me to be certain my needs were being met, and sparing very little conversation save to assure me he was working on the case. \lquote Applying pressure\rquote 
 to the young lord, in his words. He was calm, certain, professional. He seemed very sure the Heart Thief would not leave the city without us finding out. To be fair, up until now they\rquote 
d only taken buggies and coaches, at least as far as we knew. There was nothing sugges
ting they were a seasoned traveler who could orienteer in such rural country, let alone with the weather as dreadful as it had been. And going on foot would have been a death sentence.
\par I wanted to believe Malachi, and I should have been content to defer to him, at this point. I had done- quite literally- more than I was sanctioned to. But it wasn\rquote 
t just my prayers that left me feeling adrift, unfinished, balanced precariously on the edge of panic\'85
\par Up until now, my pursuit of Darcy had been in my own hands. I was given to believe no one in the Ministry thought I was on the right track, or at least that they weren\rquote t taking my reports seriously, but apparently that was not the case. They
\rquote d sent a seasoned Inquisitor, (as they should have considering the gravity of this case and this person\rquote s crimes) and I was only still a part of all of this because he seemed to think I would be useful to him while he worked on it.
\par Malachi had been more complimentary and hospitable to me than I deserved, especially given it was my }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid5052418 repeated}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
 failures that had let our quarry slip, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid5052418 again}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 . He didn\rquote t seem to hold that against me. He didn\rquote 
t expect me to be as competent as he was\'85
\par A selfish, immature part of me felt it was patronizing, but I knew well enough to check those feelings. They were wrong and I was wrong for feeling them, and ungrateful in the extreme. Malachi was an experienced
 mentor, a senior member of the Ministry being gracious enough to show me how this sort of work ought to be done, and I\rquote d be a fool to buck the help I\rquote d so desperately been asking for, all this time.
\par But\'85 the way he did things\'85
\par I wasn\rquote t completely simple, I knew much of his demeanor, the way he talked to people, was theater. These were the intimidation tactics, the social manipulation we relied upon in our line of work to get most of what we were after from people }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid8929043 without}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  the need for interrogation or force. In a way, it was a mercy. A kindness.
\par I just wasn\rquote t used to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid8929043 seeing}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  it, and it was a shocking awakening. I\rquote d witnessed 
hints of the cunning, the investigative eye, the watchfulness, in Father Helstrom. The man had been a storied Inquisitor in his day, and you didn\rquote t lose that edge just because you went into retirement. But still. I couldn\rquote 
t imagine him doing }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid15297726 half}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  the things Malachi had done in his short conversation with Cillian\'85
 even going so far as to knock him off-balance on a precarious staircase\'85
\par But realistically, he probably had. And worse.
\par This was the world I had only been given glimpses of through the hardness in my Father\rquote s eyes. Or the canny way Brother Dolus always seemed to know everything that happened at Ebon Gables, even when he\rquote 
d been away for weeks. The Ministry only accepted those who endured years of rigorous training and conditioning for the work they\rquote d be tasked with, and this one day I\rquote d gone through made it clear just how immature I was compared to 
all of them.
\par I was in over my head on this case in }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid6503040 so}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
 many ways. Brother Malachi was just trying to protect me, and get the job done. If I found it all overwhelming, it\rquote s because\'85 it was. This was beyond my abilities and experience. I had to trust him more, and doubt less.
\par It was just so hard, when every part of me seized up at the thought of what came next. Even physically, sometimes. My body was reacting to my anxieties in ways it never had before. I was fair certain it was part of what was making and keeping me sick.

\par I wanted- needed this all to be over. But I simultaneously dreaded what that would necessitate. And I was ringing myself out over the implications of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid9797072 why}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  I felt that dread.
\par For once in my life, giving Confession had not helped alleviate my guilt, nor given me any kind of peace. I could not feel God\rquote s grace, nor any clear direction. I felt so\'85
 apart from Him. More removed from His guidance than I could ever remember feeling.
\par I wanted my Father. I needed\'85 help. Maybe not Confession, but to\'85 talk. I needed\'85
\par On the second day of snow, lighter now but still falling, Malachi left me after breakfast to make for the Lane house again. He was meeting Cillian this time apparently, to question the family further. 
\par Earnestly, I was glad not to be going along for this trip. It was impossible that Darcy would be foolish enough to still be there, and he was the only person in this case I wanted to speak to again. What\rquote s more, the 
interrogation was certain to be thick with animosity, and I didn\rquote t know how to talk to a man like Lord Rathborne without making a fool of myself. The only Pedigree family I\rquote d ever really had any experience with were the Hudsons, and they
\rquote d been cordial and hospitable, but very clear on propriety and standing in their home. They\rquote 
d kept a respectful distance in our working relationship, not involved me overly much in their family affairs, and regarded me on about the same level as a trustworthy servant.
\par Whatever happened today at the Lane Estate was going to be unpleasant, if the brief interaction Malachi and Cillian Rathborne had already shared was any indication. And it didn\rquote t need to involve me. In fact, my presence would be a detriment.
\par It was cowardly I suppose, but I was happy to leave it to Malachi. He\rquote d informed me the night before that he had some leads on the connections the Lanes and the Rathbornes had in the city, and where they might be sheltering the Heart Thief. I
\rquote d spent what time I hadn\rquote t been sleeping over the last day in the Archives, pouring over information on Cillian, his family and their history, and while I\rquote d learned a lot, none of it would really help me catch Darcy.
 I was still firmly at a dead end. 
\par I had to trust Malachi would find information I could not, at this point. Or it would be back to canvasing all over the map in the adjoining towns, until I found another sighting. And that felt like going backwards. 
\par I decided that day to walk to Saint Rhine\rquote s. It was barely a few blocks and the snow was only lightly flurrying any more. I\rquote d taken my \lquote medicinal\rquote 
 tea for the day and while the cough was being stubborn, I was hardly ill enough that I couldn\rquote t get a little air and exercise. 
\par I bundled myself up and emerged into the blinding, pristine perfection of a city painted in glittering white. Redcoven had been beautiful before, but the fresh coat of soft, downy snow had settled into every unsightly crevice. It 
sparkled in the previously bare-limbed trees and tinted every shadow blue-grey. It was ethereal, and mostly undisturbed. Like walking through the kingdom of Canus in the heavens.
\par My borrowed mule was in the stables at the Church and she was being well cared-for, I was certain, but I decided I\rquote d go to check on her first. While we hadn\rquote t exactly taken to one another, she\rquote 
d done the hard work of getting me here despite my own failings at land navigation. I\rquote d had an apple with breakfast today, and saved half of it for her. She\rquote d earned it, and even the lowliest of God\rquote s creatures deserv
ed gratitude for their labors.
\par Besides, she\rquote d likely be returned to her Parish once the snow was cleared, and I wanted to say goodbye. 
\par My breath puffed out around me past the thin barrier of the lilac scarf Mabel had knitted me the year before. Purple was the color of bravery, she\rquote 
d said. I felt a smile tug at my muzzle as I remembered our Solstice Day celebration together. Nicholas had narrowly bested me in a rope tug contest, and indulged too much in the cask of hard cider he\rquote d been awarded by the Brothe
rs. Father Helstrom let him off the hook for the indiscretion, given the holiday. A truly rare grace from our otherwise stern Father. We\rquote 
d all gathered in the choir as the sun set and sung hymns for hours. Even Nicholas, which had led to many slurred lines and bouts of laughter from our younger brothers and sisters.
\par I missed my family. 
\par It was natural to miss one\rquote s home after being away from it for so long, I supposed. The last time I\rquote d spent so much time away from the Abbey had been in this city as well, when I\rquote 
d come here for training. And now I knew a Senior Brother here, an Inquisitor, who called this place home. Maybe this was all a sign. Maybe Redcoven could become a second home for me, in time. I could make acquaintances with other Brothers at Saint Rhine
\rquote s, perhaps even friends in the city. I\rquote d never really had friends outside of my brothers and sisters at the Abbey.
\par But this was part of growing up. Expanding one\rquote s circles, and knowledge. And if I really wanted to become someone like Malachi, I\rquote d have
 to be prepared to travel and bring the Word to many corners of the world. Or at least many different Parishes in Amuresca.
\par These thoughts were pleasantly distracting, and briefly eased the tensions I\rquote d been wrestling with the last two days. There was no sin in thinking about one\rquote s future, especially as a means to calm a disordered mind. So, I continued thinking 
on the possibilities, taking in the sights of the city, bedecked in its winter finery, as I walked those few blocks.
\par I arrived at the stables adjacent to the Ministry Office, where I\rquote 
d left my mule in the care of the stable boy a few days earlier. She was the first to greet me on my left, eagerly rustling inside and tucking her head out the top of her door, ears twisting forward. 
\par \'93Oh-ho,\'94 I snuffed, \'93happy to see me, are you? Where was that enthusiasm when we were on the road? Might it perhaps be attributed to the apple in my pocket?\'94
\par She flicked exactly one ear, and looked offended at my accusation.
\par \'93No, of course not,\'94 I rolled my eyes. \'93You\rquote re just darling, aren\rquote t you? How dare I defame your character.\'94
\par I produced the apple and held it out to her on my palm, patting her cheek as she crunched into it. While she ate, I briefly glanced down the row of stable doors to mentally apologize to our onlookers. I\rquote d only brought the one apple half.
\par They were in fact\'85 all looking at us. Enviously. But one familiar face caught my attention immediately and sent a bolt of shock down my spine.
\par \'93Dragonfly?\'94 I blinked, making sure I was seeing correctly. But there was no mistaking the palomino mare, I\rquote 
d been taking care of her on and off since I was thirteen. She was honey-colored with a forked white stripe down the bridge of her nose and a cream-colored mane. The stripe in particular was distinctive because of its shape.
\par I patted the mule once more and headed down towards the mare, leaning past her attempt to nuzzle at my pocket, and looked into the stall to make absolutely certain. 
\par \'93It }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid15014062 is}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  you,\'94
 I said, quietly. I leaned back out and stroked her nose, while she tried to nibble on my scarf. \'93Then, someone from home is\'85\'94
\par \'93Brother Isidor.\'94
\par I clenched my paws reflexively at the unexpected voice, worrying at the knuckle of my missing finger. I turned towards the silhouette at the other end of the stables, starkly outlined against the glaring white of the sunlit snow outside.
\par The tall canine made his way towards me unhurriedly, shrugging back the hood of his fur-lined winter robe. He wasn\rquote t dressed like he\rquote d been outside for long though. He\rquote d probably just thrown it on recently
, judging by the fact that it was still open in the front. He wore civilian dress beneath, weathered and nondescript, which I wasn\rquote t used to seeing on him, but I knew he often dressed in secular garb when he was working for the Ministry.
\par \'93Brother Dolus,\'94 I said, almost stammering for a moment as I was still in shock. I just hadn\rquote t been expecting, of all people\'85 
\par \'93I saw you through the window,\'94 he explained, \'93walking this direction.\'94
\par I straightened up as he approached and made to stand at attention, but he quickly waved a palm at me. \'93Please. No need, Brother. In fact, outside of our Abbey, it is better you regard me as no one of importance.\'94
\par \'93That\'85 would be difficult for me, Brother,\'94 I said, trying to settle as he\rquote d asked and appear more casual.
\par \'93Try,\'94 he said simply, in that dry way he did when what he was saying was actually a command, but he didn\rquote t want to come off that way
. Father Helstrom was more stern, more outwardly demanding, but in a way I preferred it. Brother Dolus could be hard to understand and thus hard to please, sometimes.
\par \'93Why have you come to Redcoven, Brother?\'94 I asked the obvious question.
\par \'93Oh come now, Isidor,\'94 he said, \'93you\rquote re a sharp lad, I\rquote m certain you can land on the answer there without my help.\'94
\par \'93The Heart Thief,\'94 I said, because there was no other explanation that made sense. \'93But\'85 so, the Ministry must have sent you? Because I\rquote ve sent many letters to Father Helstrom, but he can\rquote 
t have heard we found them here, a few days ago\'85 not in time for you to have arrived on horseback.\'94
\par Dragonfly was a sprightly, swift young mare. An Inquisitor\rquote s horse, perfect for travel for one fellow with a good seat, in just about any weather. But even still, despite the circuitous path I\rquote 
d taken in my travels to get here, by a straight shot it should have taken him\'85 four, five days? If he rode }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid8541546 hard}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 . And a good carriage would have been swifter. 
\par But more noticeable, more traceable. And more likely to get held up in the poor weather. Brother Dolus always traveled on horseback. Like the Knights and Crusaders of old. All the more impressive because he must have caught 
the tail end of the snowstorm, and he\rquote d }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid10056071 still}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
 navigated the roads and made the trip safely for himself and Dragonfly.
\par \'93I\rquote m here by my own choice,\'94 he answered my question about who\rquote d sent him and why, simply but evasively. Answering without really answering. \'93
As this investigation involves my most promising son, you can hardly blame me for worrying, can you?\'94 He approached closer, reaching a hand up and pressing his fingers into the stained, shaved-down spot at the base of my skull where I\rquote 
d had a stitch or two put in. He made a displeased noise in his muzzle. \'93Right I was to do so, apparently. This is from the incident at the Lane Estate? Any issues with your vision, or dizzy spells since?\'94
\par \'93No, sir,\'94 I responded, tipping my head back so he could inspect the minor injury better. \'93Just a slight fall.\'94
\par If he knew what had happened at the Lane Estate, it was safe to say he\rquote d been entirely caught up, either by the Ministry or Malachi, or both. Hardly surprising.
\par \'93When did you get in?\'94 I asked.
\par \'93Late yesterday,\'94 he said. \'93I took my rest at a shrine that night, never you fear. Avoided most of the storm. I knew you were in the city, and where you were keeping residence,\'94 he anticipated me before I could ask, \'93
but I was assured you were on bedrest for\'85 this\'85 and another of your trifling colds. You really must begin taking better care of yourself, son. Remember, you represent an investment of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid12977201 countless}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  hours of schooling and training, as well as other resources, that the Ministry can hardly afford to waste.
 If not concern for your own well-being, think on that the next time you go off half-cocked and behave recklessly.\'94 His jowls turned up. \'93Or think on me and how I shall have to suffer Father Helstrom\rquote 
s agonizing if something should happen to you. That bloodhound can }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid10056071 agonize}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 , let me tell you.\'94
\par \'93Father Helstrom,\'94 I said, cautiously optimistic, \'93is he-\'93
\par \'93One of us needed to tend to the flock, son,\'94 he said. \'93Besides, the man is retired, let his old bones find some rest, will you? No, I have come alone, I\rquote m afraid. But is that not enough already?\'94
\par \'93Of course sir.\'94
\par \'93This vexing case,\'94 he sighed irritably, \'93has claimed enough of the Ministry\rquote s resources as it is. Now, at least, they seem intent on settling the matter with haste. Better we see to it that it is done
, swiftly and with no expenses spared, than languish for a further few years.\'94
\par \'93I\'85 agree,\'94 I nodded.
\par \'93Good,\'94 he replied curtly. \'93I\rquote ll be assisting the Ministry and the Inquisitor here, going forward. And you, Brother, may focus on your recovery and ready yourself to return home.\'94
\par \'93With all due respect, sir,\'94 I said, because I\rquote d felt this coming and I was as ready for it as I could be, \'93I am one of those resources that cannot be spared from the case. I know the details of the crime and the criminal themselve
s, intimately. Even Brother Malachi has said he\rquote s found my assistance indispensable.\'94
\par The Inquisitor\rquote s name brought a sudden frown to the monk\rquote s features, and a darkness to his gaze I had seen very few times throughout my life, but I remembered well. Brother Dolus did not look angry often. But when he did, he was fearsome.

\par \'93Are you comfortable, Brother Isidor?\'94 he asked me unexpectedly. \'93Keeping residence with Brother Malachi?\'94
\par \'93Yes,\'94 I said, uncertainty creeping into my voice until the word was almost a question. \'93He\'85 has been very kind, hospitable and fair with me.\'94
\par \'93Mmh,\'94 he replied, neutrally. There was a pause, long enough that it began to grow uncomfortable between us, and I could feel his eyes }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid6386214 digging}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  in to me. The boys back home called it \lquote The Inquisitor\rquote s Stare\rquote 
. Father Helstrom was less subtle about it, more prone to exercise it, and it almost always bore confessions or results. With Brother Dolus, it was rarer\'85 but no less effective.
\par  I didn\rquote t know what he wanted me to say. I stammered silently over a few things, but only one real indiscretion came to mind and good Lord, I did not want to talk about that with him.
\par \'93Take care in how much you\'85 absorb\'85 from Brother Malachi, lad,\'94 Brother Dolus at last said, his expression softening. \'93He has a long and troubled record of disciplinary issues and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid6106269 serious}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  infractions of conduct, failings of character I would rather you not emulate, or think are normal for us in this kind of work.\'94
\par \'93Such as?\'94 I asked, venturing the question quietly so it wouldn\rquote t sound too demanding.
\par \'93Brother,\'94 he chided, \'93that is between Brother Malachi, the Ministry, and God. You know that.\'94
\par \'93Ah,\'94 I swallowed, nervously.
\par \'93\'85 but I will say this much,\'94 he murmured. \'93These were not minor infractions. He is a sinner, Isidor. Washed in the waters of redemption for his transgressions, forgiven by the Ministry and God\'85
 but not without much time and blood paid through re-education and penance. That is not something I would want to see one of my sons endure.\'94
\par \'93\rquote Re-education\rquote ?\'94 I\rquote d never heard of something like that. How was one \lquote re-educated\rquote ?
\par \'93A work camp, Isidor,\'94 he explained quietly. \'93Malachi made some very grave mistakes in his youth, and he paid dearly for them. He is not a man to aspire to.\'94
\par My mouth was dry, my hand ached for the empty joint it had lost, but something in me }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid4346598 could}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  not be still, }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid4346598 could}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  not be sated, until I\rquote 
d put to words the voice that was demanding to be heard from the recesses of my mind.
\par \'93If he has truly overcome such bone-deep sin,\'94 I said, \'93and endured his penance, to prove himself now in the eyes of the Ministry as worthy of his title, despite it all\'85 then I actually think that is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid10056071 very}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  aspirational.\'94
\par Brother Dolus was a mild-mannered man, and sometimes emotions could be hard to pick out in his features. But there was a hint of genuine shock following my reply to him. 
\par I waited for it to break. I waited for anger, or disappointment, or worse yet, that coldness he could exude. But instead, he\'85 laughed.
\par A rasp, breathless enough that I knew it was real. He even closed his eyes a moment, staring down at the straw-littered planks, and let the chuckles shake his shoulders.
\par \'93Oh, how I wish Germain were here for that,\'94 he said almost to himself, before looking back up at me. \'93Some time in the world, fending for yourself on the roads, has finally hardened you up some from the chubby little pup you were, Isidor.\'94

\par \'93Sir?\'94 I balked.
\par \'93You\rquote re becoming a man, son,\'94 he said. \'93Standing up to your father, or the nearest to it, in any case\'85\'94
\par Father Helstrom was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid3301225 literally}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  my Father, but I didn\rquote t dare say that out loud.
\par \'93Alright, I know when I\rquote m beat. You\rquote re fixated on this and that\rquote s fine. Stay in the city a few weeks longer if you really like,\'94 he said, waving a hand. \'93And if you enjoy Brother Malachi\rquote s company, so be it. I\rquote 
m certain at the very least he\rquote ll have plenty of busywork for you to do at the Archives, and you\rquote re a bit of a book worm, so you ought to enjoy that. It\rquote s hardly as though we\rquote 
re in a rush to get you on another assignment. If you\rquote d like to see this one through to the end, you\rquote re safe enough in Redcoven with all of us here.\'94
\par \'93I\rquote d like that,\'94 I said, letting out a breath. \'93I was worried you were going to send me back to the Hudsons\'85\'94
\par \'93Oh, no, that opportunity has passed,\'94 he said dismissively. \'93And taken on quite eagerly by another young Templar very happy to find their place in such a fine household. Young Nicholas knew a good posting when he saw one.\'94
\par \'93Nick?!\'94 I blurted out. \'93How\'85 he isn\rquote t even ordained\'85\'94
\par \'93He is training for the position with Templar Olvar, and will succeed him when he retires,\'94 he said, cocking his head.  \'93Oh, don\rquote t get envious now, Brother Isidor. You had your chance.\'94
\par \'93No, no,\'94 I insisted, unable to mask my shock. \'93It\rquote s just, he\rquote s only fifteen-\'93
\par \'93Our Parish is short on well-trained, unmarried Templar who are available for posting,\'94 he said. \'93We can only bring up so many boys, Isidor\'85 as I said, it takes resources. The Hudsons do not prefer a married Templar on their grounds;
 their lady objects to the presence of any young, low-bred woman so near to the estate\'85 and her husband. In retrospect, this works out well. It\rquote 
s an ideal, easy post for a young Templar to cut his teeth on, for five years or so until he is ready to marry. Nicholas will do well there. And you\'85\'94
\par I hadn\rquote t interrupted him. He\rquote d just trailed off, like he was considering how to phrase what he meant to say. Which made me nervous.
\par \'93You\'85 are a passionate young man, Brother,\'94 he settled on. \'93And those passions need be redirected, I think. Before they incite you to troublesome behavior.\'94
\par \'93Have I done something?\'94 I asked nervously. What did he know? How did he know it?
\par \'93No, lad,\'94 he chuckled again. \'93I\rquote m just saying, I believe it is time you marry. You can finish out this case, but afterwards, before you enter your training with the Ministry, would be a
n ideal time. There is nothing preventing you from being a married man when you apply, it will not hurt your chances. And you\rquote ll be sent to the Ministry grounds in Amurfolk 
for your education, they provide very adequate apartments for keeping a wife.\'94 
\par \'93I-\'93 I almost sputtered, \'93-I\rquote m not\'85 I-I\rquote m not even twenty, yet.\'94
\par \'93Yes, and if you were one of your sisters, you\rquote d be a spinster already,\'94 he said. \'93Some young men prefer not to marry until they are more seasoned, more experienced with the world. But Isidor, you are not moneyed\'85
 you are not weighing many prospects\'85 it isn\rquote t as though extended courtships would do you any benefit. And moreover, you are simply not the sort of young man who }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid5270853 
should}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  wait, in my own opinion.\'94
\par That absolutely begged the question, \'93Why?\'94
\par He gave me a long, even stare. \'93Very well. Isidor, I am not Father Helstrom. I do not mind being indelicate with you, if I must. You are, as I said before, a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid1901879 passionate}
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  young man. For some of us, the fires burn a little hotter in our youth.\'94
\par Oh. I had an idea what he meant, now\'85
\par \'93You struggle with lust,\'94 and there, he had just outright stated it. I felt my ears burn and couldn\rquote t hold his gaze any longer. \'93And before you ask, I do not need to take Confession from you to know. Father Helstrom speaks to me in 
how best to counsel you, because he falls woefully short in knowing how to address these matters.\'94 He sighed. \'93I do not know what he has told you that may have been of any help\'85 but please, allow me to offer my own\'85
 more educated viewpoint. Father Helstrom means well, but he does not understand your struggles, in this sin at least. Trust me.\'94 He was very certain-sounding on that point.
\par \'93As you say, sir,\'94 I mumbled.
\par \'93These feelings of yours,\'94 he continued, \'93they are not unnatural, they are not heretical, they are }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid1901879 vigor}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 . It speaks to your potency, in fact. You\rquote ll sire many sons someday, Isidor.\'94
\par \'93\'85 really?\'94 I asked after a few moments, tipping my ears up marginally.
\par \'93Yes,\'94 he said, shaking his head with a smile that seemed genuine. \'93Desire if directed appropriately is part of God\rquote 
s design for man. It is right and natural that you should feel these things, should want for them, even outside the bounds of marriage. The sin would be acting upon them before you\rquote ve sealed your union. That is why it is }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid12389408 so}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  important t
hat young men like you, with health and vigor, and a respectful demeanor that will certainly temper those flames when you are at last consummating your marriage, marry young. Before you have faltered, and given way to temptation.\'94
\par I found myself nodding, murmuring a quiet, \'93I\'85 I do understand.\'94
\par It made sense, everything he was saying. I wasn\rquote t sure why it still felt wrong to me. Or at the least, why I was so uncertain.
\par \'93Think of a marriage as a hearth,\'94 he said, clasping my hands in his. \'93Properly contained\'85 safe\'85
 a fire can warm a home, and foster a family. But allowed to burn out of control, without restraint, it can destroy. You need to build your hearth, son. Soon will be the right time to do so. Think of the life you can have, training with the Ministry
 in Amurfolk, being forged into a sword worthy of the Inquisition. This is what you wanted, yes? Father Helstrom and I will support you. Amurfolk would be an excellent city to break in a wife, too. You\rquote d have all the guidance and support you
\rquote d need from Senior Brothers who\rquote ve navigated their own marriages.\'94
\par He released my hands, and I found myself staring down at them for a time following. I flexed all nine fingers.
\par He seemed to realize, then glanced back down himself, giving me a fond look. \'93Ah yes, my \lquote Southpaw\rquote . You\rquote ve distinguished yourself so well already, Isidor. I\rquote m very proud, you know, of the man you\rquote 
ve become. And you were not blessed well from birth, so it is especially impressive you\rquote ve come so far.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid12866453 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1647866 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 \tab \'93Thank you, sir,\'94 I said.
\par \tab \'93Father Helstrom sent his last letter with me,\'94 he said, reaching to his shabby vest pocket to pull the carefully-wrapped and sealed letter out. \'93No sense wasting a courier. I think this one is replying to one of your letters from\'85
 over a week ago though, so it may be out of date. All the same.\'94
\par \tab I took it from him and noted the seal quickly before I stowed it in my own belt pouch. It was unbroken, the stamp bearing the seal of the \lquote wolf\rquote s head\rquote  side of our country\rquote 
s gold crown. A perfect, flawless imprint of the design.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1647866 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 Father Helstrom\rquote 
s seal had a scratch in it that crossed over the design on the left corner. A notch. The notch was missing, on this seal.
\par I said nothing, but forced a smile and thanked him. \'93Thank you for bringing it all this way,\'94 I said. \'93I\rquote ll need to write to him again, soon.\'94
\par \'93He wanted for me to sit with you for Confession, as well,\'94 he said while gesturing towards the Ministry office. \'93Shall we? I have a small room that will do.\'94
\par \'93Oh, no need,\'94 I said, before I\rquote d really thought about it. \'93I\rquote ve already\'85\'94
\par He quirked an eyebrow at me. \'93With Father Whitfield, then? Very well. I suppose I should have known you\rquote d get right to it as soon as you arrived here.\'94
\par I wanted to correct myself, then. Tell him what had happened. I needed to make proper Confession, and Brother Dolus had taken the oaths, despite not being a Priest. It was 
helpful to have two elders ordained in one Abbey who could do so, considering how large our flock was. 
He rarely made the time that Father Helstrom did to take Confession, but he was permitted to. I could have absolved myself, properly this time. I could have talked to him about what had happened with Malachi, asked him what to do, or if I\rquote 
d done anything wrong that night. How to repent, if I had.
\par But he\rquote d been so disapproving of Brother Malachi, I was afraid of what he\rquote d say. Afraid of what he might }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid15874028 do}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 . Not to me, but to him. I didn\rquote t want to upset the balance of the case.
\par Or at least, that\rquote s what I told myself.
\par \'93We should sit down all the same and discuss everything that\rquote s happened,\'94 he said, walking with me down the stalls, towards the lane outside. \'93I want you to tell me everything I haven\rquote t heard already from the Ministry.\'94
\par \'93Yes, sir,\'94 I nodded. 
\par \'93Something is bothering you, Isidor,\'94 he noted, almost idly. But it wasn\rquote t a question. \'93Please\'85 Confession aside, I am your Senior and I like to think, one of y
our fathers. At least insofar as God has blessed me. You can speak to me of what troubles you outside of the rituals, you know. I\rquote d like that, even.\'94
\par Despite his unsettling ability to know more than he should, his occasional aloofness and odd way of phrasing some of his counsel, Brother Dolus }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid7422454 was}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866  one of my mentors. As he\rquote d said\'85 a father, of a sort. I respected him, and trusted he wanted what was best for me. 
\par I took my time, trying to boil down everything that had sent me reeling over the last few days into one question. One thought that I knew I could not ask Father Whitfield, or even someone like Father Helstrom. But perhaps Brother Dolus was the righ
t person to talk to about matters like this. Because for all of my misgivings about the man, he could be more grounded, more practical, and more forgiving of mortal folly than most. He had been, and still was, an Inquisitor, and he\rquote 
d seen the worst the world had to offer in his work, I was certain. 
\par He\rquote d told me repeatedly just now how all of these struggles I\rquote d had with lust were normal, natural. He\rquote d given me advice that had honestly been\'85 sound. Even if I was still trepidatious about the idea of marriage
. To see it from that perspective, it felt\'85 less dire.
\par At the worst, he\rquote d have me give Confession. And then, at least, I could spill it all, and maybe that would fix things somehow.
\par \'93Brother,\'94 I said, hesitantly. \'93I know this is heresy, but it is also education I may eventually receive in Amurfolk, so\'85 please forgive me asking.\'94
\par \'93Much of what you will learn as an Inquisitor concerns heresy,\'94 he confirmed. \'93We can hardly investigate that which we do not understand.\'94
\par \'93How do two men sin together?\'94
\par He stopped 
walking. I nearly tripped, but stopped in time and turned towards him, worriedly. What I saw on his features was not anger or something worse, but resignation. He sighed out through his nose and stared past me, to the street. Towards the direction I
\rquote d walked from.
\par \'93I\rquote m sorry,\'94 I said quietly. \'93I shouldn\rquote t have asked\'85 I\rquote m\'85 I only asked because-\'93
\par \'93I am unfortunately already very aware of why you\rquote re asking me this now, Isidor,\'94 he cut me off. 
\par I shrunk back. He knew? But then, that\rquote s right, he\rquote d said he\rquote d been talking to Father Helstrom about my issues with lust. Which meant my dreams. Which meant he 
knew or at least suspected the Heart Thief was involved, and he also likely knew that we\rquote d recently learned they were a man, and-
\par \'93I think you should move your residence to the Ministry Offices for now,\'94 he said. \'93Or Saint Rhine\rquote s. Either is fine.\'94
\par \'93Why?\'94 I blinked, confused.
\par \'93Because I\rquote m not risking one of my boys, leaving them in the care of that man, that\rquote s why,\'94 he said, his voice hard. \'93Absolutely no arguing on this point, Isidor. Understood? Tonight. Get your things together.\'94
\par He was worried about\'85
\par \'93Malachi?\'94 I asked, shocked. \'93You\rquote re worried about my staying at his residence? No\'85 I\rquote m asking for my own clarification-\'93
\par \'93Isidor, I know who put these thoughts in your head,\'94 he said tiredly. \'93Please do not try to protect the man.\'94
\par He wasn\rquote t entirely wrong, but still. \'93It\rquote s not like what you\rquote re inferring,\'94 I tried to insist.
\par \'93If one of my boys was a sodomite, I would know,\'94 he said, his tone leaving no room for error. \'93Especially you, Isidor. I mean no offense when I say this, but it is the truth. 
I have never raised a boy more prone to vomiting up his feelings and every inner thought at any given chance and time. Females, certainly. But you wear your heart on your sleeve, and give Confession more often than most monks.\'94 He sighed. \'93
Trust me more than you do yourself, lad. I would know.\'94
\par \'93\rquote Sodomite\rquote ?\'94 I said after a moment. I knew the word vaguely, in an academic sense, but surely he didn\rquote t mean-
\par \'93They breed one another in the arse, lad,\'94 he growled out. \'93Or the mouth. Like whores.\'94
\par I stared at him in dumb silence, aghast.
\par He patted me on the shoulder, moving past. \'93Yes, that\rquote s the proper response. Horror. You see? You\rquote re fine. But I suppose you\rquote re right, you had to get an education sooner or later, so there you have it. Now, come. I\rquote 
d rather be discussing the sodomite Pedigree fellow and his little man-cunt thief, yes? Let\rquote s get back to that, so we can be done with this case, and done with this city.\'94
\par 
\par On the way back to Malachi\rquote s apartment, late into the afternoon, considering how I was going to explain to the big fellow that my Senior Monk didn\rquote t think I was safe with him\'85 I remembered my prayer book.
\par The cache Darcy had told me of. It was actually one of the few things I\rquote d learned of in speaking with them that, for whatever reason, I hadn\rquote t shared with anyone else yet. It wasn\rquote t even in my report to the Ministry.
\par I\rquote d report it eventually, I told myself. But it was my prayer book, it was personal. And I wanted to be certain I got my hands on it first, if Darcy had in fact been telling the truth.
\par And then I was on a mission, making my way through the city with purpose, towards the spot she\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid10056071 he}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
 had told me he\rquote d hidden it away. Why I hadn\rquote t gone for it earlier, I could hardly justify.
\par Oh, right. I\rquote d been injured and sick.
\par I was still sick, the cough stubbornly reminding me of its existence, stuck in my lungs like tar. And rushing through the city streets in the waning hours, the air growing colder by the minute, certainly did it no favors.
\par But all I could think of was getting my prayer book back. 
\par It isn\rquote 
t even as though there was any particular wisdom in there that would shed light on anything going on in my life right now. The prayers were all those I could read in my Tome of Faith, just selectively-picked for easy reading. And my journalling was\'85 we
ll, all from within. Nothing to be had there I hadn\rquote t already ruminated over hundreds of times before.
\par But I wanted it. I wanted it because\'85 to hell with it, I could admit it to myself. I wanted it because of its connection to him. I wanted it because he\rquote d left it for me, he\rquote d returned it to me, and maybe, just maybe, he\rquote d\'85

\par He\rquote d written in the margins of the novels he\rquote d left me. Maybe.
\par Maybe.
\par I had to dig through a crust of ice and a foot of collected snow to claw down into the hollow of the tree, and a mass of frozen dirt, leaves and debris, and then\'85 the potato sack was frozen, because of course it was. But it was there, just as he
\rquote d said it would be.
\par I plopped down into the tangled nest of snow outside the hollow after I\rquote d finally, at long length, plied the small leather-wrapped book free. It was really there, it was solid and cold in my hand, he hadn\rquote t lied to me\'85
\par The spine cracked when I opened it, and my breath left my lungs on the very first page, because\'85 it was exactly as I\rquote d left it. Nothing additional there.
\par I flipped one page, then another, my shoulders slumping slowly as I went. What had I honestly been hoping for, here? Was I mad?
\par And then\'85 and then\'85
\par Red ink.
\par Halfway in, on one of the pages I\rquote d written in long ago, in a few spare pages between the psalms on forgiveness. He\rquote 
d written in calligraphy, elegant and beautiful, and decorated with a stylized, simple twisting pen pattern that resembled a dove in the top left corner. As if it were a bookmark, begging my attention.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid13585365 \lquote I like your hand-writing. It\rquote s unusual.\rquote }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866 I kept reading. And then I flipped furiously through the rest of it, looking for more. He had only written on the one page. But he\rquote 
d written a whole paragraph. Not just about my handwriting.
\par I could have cried.
\par I gasped, struggling to breathe, coughed, swallowed, and read it again. And again.
\par God, I needed to find him. God, please.
\par I needed to find him before they did.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid1647866\charrsid13585365 
